= Bayesian inference — Simple =

'''Bayesian inference''' is a way to update probabilities when new evidence arrives.

You start with a '''prior''', which represents what was plausible before the new evidence.

Then you use a '''likelihood''', which says how expected the evidence would be under different possibilities.

Combining them gives a '''posterior''', which represents the updated probabilities.

A simple summary is:

<pre>
prior × likelihood → posterior
</pre>

Bayes' theorem is the mathematical rule behind this update, but Bayesian inference also requires choosing a model and checking whether it fits the problem.

